  "description": "A parametric POTTED PLANT on the six-node spine, and the replacement for the faceted green dome in view1.\n\nDROP IT ON A NODE. `plant` treats a canopy as one solid; this one draws leaves, because no material fixes a silhouette.\n\nEACH LEAF IS A KITE IN PLAN — stem, two shoulders, tip — swept a leaf-thickness in z. The kernel takes any quad, so a leaf costs exactly what a box costs, and eighteen of them at staggered heights and angles give a plant an outline.\n\nTHE ANGLES ARE PHYLLOTAXIS, NOT A GRID. 137.5 degrees between successive leaves is what a plant actually does, and it fills a circle without any two ever lining up — which is precisely the artefact an evenly-spaced fan would have.\n\nTHE DROOP IS IN WHERE THE LEAF STARTS, not in its own plane: a quad sweeps straight up, so a truly tilted leaf is not expressible here. Read from across a room, the two agree; read from 200 mm away they do not, and this object says so rather than pretending.\n\nFULLNESS IS LEAF AREA OVER CANOPY AREA — whether it reads full or sparse, which a leaf count alone cannot tell you because the leaves vary.\n\nDIMENSIONS AT THE DEFAULTS: a 320 mm pot, 450 mm trunk, 18 leaves in 3 tiers, canopy about 900 mm across and 1.2 m tall.",
